---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// V-700 — migration guide for Puppeteer / Playwright users.
---

<BaseLayout title="Migrate from Puppeteer / Playwright — Driftstack" description="Mapping Puppeteer/Playwright concepts to the Driftstack API and an incremental migration path.">
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12 prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert">
    <h1>Migrate from Puppeteer / Playwright</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      Driftstack runs the same WebKit you're driving locally, but as
      a managed service with persistent profile state, retention-
      managed recordings, and per-account isolation. This page maps
      Puppeteer / Playwright concepts to Driftstack equivalents and
      walks through an incremental migration.
    </p>

    <h2>Why migrate</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No browser-fleet management.</strong> No headless
        Chrome containers to keep alive, no Selenium grid to patch,
        no Mac mini farm to babysit.</li>
      <li><strong>Profile persistence.</strong> Cookies / localStorage
        / IndexedDB live in a server-side encrypted profile (V-312)
        — clone, snapshot, restore through the API.</li>
      <li><strong>Recordings + retention.</strong> Every session can
        be recorded as WebM with tier-dependent retention; presigned
        playback URLs are HTTP-friendly.</li>
      <li><strong>Concurrency caps as a feature.</strong> Tier-driven
        concurrent-session limits + queue feedback let you scale
        traffic without over-provisioning.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Concept mapping</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Puppeteer / Playwright</th><th>Driftstack</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>puppeteer.launch()</code> / <code>browser.newContext()</code></td>
          <td><code>POST /v1/sessions</code> — creates a fresh session bound to a profile.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>page.goto(url)</code></td>
          <td><code>POST /v1/sessions/:id/navigate</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>page.click(selector)</code> / <code>page.type</code></td>
          <td><code>POST /v1/sessions/:id/interact</code> with action +
            selector. <code>POST /v1/sessions/:id/gui-input</code> for
            keyboard / pointer events at coordinates.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>page.waitFor(...)</code></td>
          <td><code>POST /v1/sessions/:id/wait</code> with a strategy
            (selector / network-idle / fixed timeout).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>page.evaluate</code></td>
          <td><code>GET /v1/sessions/:id/state</code> returns the
            current DOM + URL + cookies snapshot. Arbitrary script
            eval is intentionally not exposed.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>page.screenshot()</code></td>
          <td><code>POST /v1/sessions/:id/capture</code> with
            <code>kind=screenshot</code>. Returns inline base64
            bytes (no presigned URL).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>browser.close()</code></td>
          <td><code>DELETE /v1/sessions/:id</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Userdata-dir (<code>--user-data-dir=...</code>)</td>
          <td>Profile resource (<code>POST /v1/profiles</code>).
            Profiles bind cookies / localStorage / IndexedDB to a
            named archetype; sessions inherit the binding when they
            run against the profile.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Saving + loading session.json</td>
          <td>Profile snapshot
            (<code>POST /v1/profiles/:id/snapshots</code>) +
            restore (<code>POST /v1/profile-snapshots/:id/restore</code>).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>browser.tracing.start({ path })</code> / video record</td>
          <td>Roadmap — see
            <a href="/docs/recordings">/docs/recordings</a>. Until
            recordings ship, drive observation with per-step
            <code>capture</code> screenshots / DOM snapshots.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Test parallelism via shards</td>
          <td>Concurrent-session cap per tier. The
            <code>concurrency_limit_reached</code> 429 is your back-
            pressure signal — back off + retry.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Side-by-side: a typical login + scrape</h2>
    <p><strong>Puppeteer:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>{`const browser = await puppeteer.launch();
const page = await browser.newPage();
await page.goto('https://app.example.com/login');
await page.type('#email', 'user@example.com');
await page.type('#password', SECRET);
await page.click('button[type=submit]');
await page.waitForSelector('#dashboard');
const data = await page.$eval('#kpi', el => el.textContent);
await browser.close();`}</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Driftstack (curl):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>{`POST /v1/sessions
  { "archetype": "default", "label": "qa-login" }
  → 201 Created   { "id": "ses_…", "status": "creating", … }

POST /v1/sessions/ses_…/navigate
  { "url": "https://app.example.com/login" }

POST /v1/sessions/ses_…/interact
  { "action": "type", "selector": "#email", "value": "user@example.com" }
POST /v1/sessions/ses_…/interact
  { "action": "type", "selector": "#password", "value": "{{SECRET}}" }
POST /v1/sessions/ses_…/interact
  { "action": "click", "selector": "button[type=submit]" }

POST /v1/sessions/ses_…/wait
  { "kind": "selector", "selector": "#dashboard", "timeout_ms": 10000 }

GET /v1/sessions/ses_…/state
  → { "html": "…", "url": "…", "cookies": [...] }

DELETE /v1/sessions/ses_…`}</code></pre>

    <h2>What's not directly equivalent</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt>Arbitrary <code>page.evaluate</code> script execution</dt>
      <dd>Not exposed — the API surface is action-based. If you need
        a specific extraction primitive (e.g. "return the value of
        attribute X on selector Y"), it can usually be expressed via
        <code>state</code> + client-side post-processing. For
        complex eval needs, contact us — we sometimes carve out a
        targeted endpoint.</dd>

      <dt>Direct browser-process introspection (CDP, devtools)</dt>
      <dd>Driftstack manages WebKit out of process; CDP isn't
        exposed. If you need network-level capture, use session
        recordings (V-540 + retention) instead — they capture every
        request body via the proxy layer.</dd>

      <dt>Synchronous chaining</dt>
      <dd>Every action is an HTTP round-trip. Total latency per
        action is ~50-150ms from a co-located client. For tight
        loops, consider whether the work can be batched into a
        single <code>state</code> call afterwards.</dd>

      <dt>Local browser plugins / extensions</dt>
      <dd>The managed browser doesn't load extensions. Stealth-style
        plugin counter-detection is handled at the driver layer +
        archetype config; you can't ship your own.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h2>Migration path</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Wrap.</strong> Keep your existing Puppeteer code;
        write a thin "driftstack-compat" wrapper that mimics
        <code>page.click</code> / <code>page.goto</code> against the
        Driftstack API. Migrate one test file at a time.</li>
      <li><strong>Profiles first.</strong> The first non-trivial
        migration win is replacing your bespoke "save cookies and
        load them on next run" logic with profile snapshots. That
        alone often deletes 50-200 lines of bespoke code.</li>
      <li><strong>Add recordings.</strong> Once a flow is on
        Driftstack, flip <code>record: true</code> on the session
        create. Now flaky-test debugging stops needing a local
        repro.</li>
      <li><strong>Drop the wrapper.</strong> When the last
        Puppeteer caller is gone, delete the compatibility shim
        and use the Driftstack SDK / CLI directly.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>SDK examples (TypeScript)</h2>
    <pre><code>{`import { Driftstack } from '@driftstack/sdk';

const client = new Driftstack({ apiKey: process.env.DRIFTSTACK_API_KEY! });

const session = await client.sessions.create({
  archetype: 'default',
  label: 'qa-login',
});
await client.sessions.navigate(session.id, { url: 'https://app.example.com/login' });
await client.sessions.interact(session.id, { action: 'type', selector: '#email', value: 'user@x.com' });
await client.sessions.interact(session.id, { action: 'click', selector: 'button[type=submit]' });
await client.sessions.wait(session.id, { kind: 'selector', selector: '#dashboard', timeout_ms: 10000 });
const state = await client.sessions.getState(session.id);
await client.sessions.destroy(session.id);`}</code></pre>

    <h2>Common gotchas</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Selectors must be stable.</strong> Puppeteer
        tolerates inline-script-generated selectors better than
        Driftstack's wait strategy (which is selector-only +
        network-idle, no DOM-mutation observer yet). Prefer
        <code>data-test-*</code> attributes on the SUT.</li>
      <li><strong>Don't sleep in your client; use
        <code>wait</code>.</strong> A naive 5-second client-side
        sleep wastes session time + session-minutes billing.</li>
      <li><strong>Destroy sessions promptly.</strong> Sessions count
        against your concurrent cap until destroyed; abandoned
        sessions block new ones. Wrap session creation in a
        try/finally.</li>
      <li><strong>API-key scopes.</strong> A read-only scrape needs
        only the <code>read</code> + <code>write</code> session
        scopes — don't mint <code>account_owner</code>-scoped keys
        for runtime use.</li>
    </ul>

